WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.167
Triton Inference Server.

2
00:00:02.467 --> 00:00:05.272
A GPU is wasted on one request at a time.

3
00:00:05.272 --> 00:00:08.967
Triton fixes that for classic machine learning models.

4
00:00:10.167 --> 00:00:14.400
Triton is NVIDIA's production serving layer for any model framework.

5
00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:20.933
ONNX, TensorRT, PyTorch, TensorFlow, even Python scripts, all behind one endpoint.

6
00:00:21.233 --> 00:00:28.400
It can also chain preprocessing, model, and postprocessing inside the server, so clients just send raw inputs.

7
00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:34.233
Triton is pointed at a model repository, which is just a directory tree.

8
00:00:34.533 --> 00:00:36.300
Each model gets its own folder.

9
00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:40.279
Inside are a config file, and numbered version folders.

10
00:00:40.279 --> 00:00:44.092
In poll mode, Triton hot loads a new version you drop in.

11
00:00:44.092 --> 00:00:46.300
The default mode won't notice it.

12
00:00:47.500 --> 00:00:50.100
Here is the killer feature, dynamic batching.

13
00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:52.533
Single requests arrive one by one.

14
00:00:52.833 --> 00:00:55.100
Triton holds them for a few milliseconds.

15
00:00:55.400 --> 00:00:57.233
It merges them into one batch.

16
00:00:57.533 --> 00:01:00.276
And the GPU processes them in a single pass.

17
00:01:00.276 --> 00:01:03.767
You trade a hair of latency for multiples of throughput.

18
00:01:04.967 --> 00:01:06.400
Here is the config file.

19
00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:10.200
It names the model and says it runs on the ONNX runtime.

20
00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:12.400
Max batch size is sixty four.

21
00:01:12.700 --> 00:01:18.600
And the queue delay of five thousand microseconds means hold requests up to five milliseconds.

22
00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:21.200
Now a real scenario.

23
00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:26.833
During Kafka telemetry bursts, hundreds of light curve windows arrive within milliseconds.

24
00:01:27.133 --> 00:01:32.967
Triton merges them into batches of forty to sixty, instead of lonely calls at batch size one.

25
00:01:33.267 --> 00:01:36.146
Throughput more than triples on the same GPU.

26
00:01:36.146 --> 00:01:40.433
And with poll mode on, version two deploys by dropping in a folder.

27
00:01:41.633 --> 00:01:43.433
One common mix up is the name.

28
00:01:43.733 --> 00:01:46.126
The inference server serves models.

29
00:01:46.126 --> 00:01:50.433
OpenAI's Triton is a separate language for writing GPU kernels.

30
00:01:51.633 --> 00:01:52.519
Let's recap.

31
00:01:52.519 --> 00:01:56.433
Triton serves a model repository behind one endpoint.

32
00:01:56.733 --> 00:02:00.433
Dynamic batching merges requests into GPU batches.

33
00:02:00.733 --> 00:02:04.367
Ensembles chain pre and post processing inside the server.

34
00:02:04.667 --> 00:02:10.038
It is the general purpose workhorse, where vLLM is for text generation.

35
00:02:10.038 --> 00:02:11.400
Next up: TensorRT.
